{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\nAgents creating tickets are not shown similar existing tickets, so duplicates accumulate and fragment work (operator ruling 2026-09-06). The first implementation, epic only-crave-art (bd83-c56f-1f72-4ef2, ADR 0086, landed 2026-07-09/10), was turned off on 2026-08-27 (`[llm] overlap_drain = \"off\"`, 62ab) and 2026-08-31 (`verify.suggest_duplicate_tickets = false`, 4eae) because its persistence and coordination design overloaded the shared tickets branch. Its code is removed by epic bossy-skittish-foal (cea1-c784-99e0-446a); this epic is the design record for the replacement.\n\n**Config state re-measured 2026-10-31.** Both switch-off citations are historical: the 13 `[llm] overlap_*` keys including `overlap_drain` are gone from `origin/main`'s `rebar.toml`, and `verify.suggest_duplicate_tickets` is `true` but **repurposed** — it now gates only the advisory create-time same-title journal, the baseline the AC1 semantic-stratum recall must beat.\n\n## Measured facts for the redesign\n\n- Store: 4,285 tickets on the hot-path snapshot, 147k event files. Ticket dirs were 8,960 when this epic was written; **9,778** on `origin/tickets` 1e4979b3308, 5,663 live. The store grows during the epic, so size targets track the live store, not a frozen number.\n- Duplicates worth catching are semantic. eac3 (2026-08-21) measured 51 pairs, 44 semantic, exact-title recall 14%. **That sample is superseded.** Re-derived at tickets branch 0b9294311b5: 1,524 pairs, but **1,431 (94%) have byte-identical normalized titles** — 84 clusters that are bulk-creation artifacts, not authored duplicates. The genuine set is the **93 differing-title pairs** (double eac3's 44, same kind). eac3's qualitative finding stands; its ratio does not. Pooling the strata would hand the cheapest lexical method near-perfect recall and reject vector search on an artifact.\n- Paraphrase retrieval: digest-BM25F got 8 of 8 heavy paraphrases at rank 1 (recall@20 = 1.0), stable from a 60- to a 200-ticket corpus, where raw-text BM25 degraded 0.875 -> 0.75 (only-crave-art derisking, N = 8).\n- Tickets are large: enrichment prompts hit 206k tokens before bounding (569c), so a semantic method needs summarization before embedding.\n- Embeddings were rejected in July on scale grounds (\"ANN pays off only >100K vectors\") with N = 8; this epic reopens that decision.\n\n## Operator framing (2026-09-06)\n\nThis is a vector search problem over summaries of large tickets. A lexical-only local index is insufficient, and provably so against the historical corpus.\n\n## Constraints every design must satisfy (14-tool OSS survey)\n\n- Derived search data never lives as records on the `tickets` branch; it is a disposable, version-keyed index outside the replicated store (git-bug keeps Bleve under `.git/git-bug/`; Cargo keys its cache by commit SHA).\n- No indexing work is spawned from the write path; catch-up happens in the reader's process on next use, keyed by the tickets-branch HEAD SHA **and a format version**. A **format-version** mismatch forces a **full rebuild** (stored data unreadable under the new schema); a **HEAD-SHA advance** is reconciled **incrementally** over only the changed ticket directories, and that path bounds AC5's cold-catch-up cost. The ADR (AC1) fixes both; c670 specifies the split. A `create` is both reader and writer: its suggestion step is a read and MAY run catch-up synchronously in-process; its event write never spawns, queues, or waits on indexing.\n- Compose-time suggestion is non-blocking and capped (3 to 5 results). **No minimum-title-length gate shipped**: `query_text` concatenates the title with the head of the description, and a query yielding no usable tokens matches nothing through `match_expression`'s `_NO_MATCH` fallback — which covers the degenerate case a length threshold was meant to.\n- Model calls are bounded and off the write path: summarization and embedding run in a batch step or the read-phase catch-up above, never as a side effect of writing an event. **The on-demand LLM judge was designed out, not built.** ADR 0086's v1 judge contract (both orderings, a required cited shared artifact, default related-but-distinct, abstain-when-unsure) is deliberately NOT carried forward: a350 instead renders at most `RESULT_LIMIT` candidates into the context of a plan-review pass that already runs, adding **zero** LLM calls. There is therefore no judge verdict vocabulary to specify: the disposition is the enclosing pass's own finding.\n- Failure disposition is advisory-degrades-never-blocks: if the index is missing, catch-up or rebuild fails, or retrieval errors, the suggestion field comes back empty or lexical-only with a diagnostic and the `create` still succeeds. The ADR records which tier serves each degraded case.\n- The ADR's embedding model must be callable from the production Bedrock role. **Discharged**: `infra/terraform/iam_s7.tf:17-21` scopes `bedrock:InvokeModel` and `InvokeModelWithResponseStream` to `anthropic.claude-*`, the Claude inference profile, **and `amazon.titan-embed-text-v2:0`**, with a live probe in `infra/runbooks/bedrock-access.md`. Only a model outside {Claude, Titan} would make adding its ARN a gating prerequisite. Retrieval as shipped is lexical and needs no model credential at all.\n- Correctness never depends on a daemon; the optional MCP server may pre-warm on a timer through the same code path.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] An ADR records the chosen retrieval architecture (summarization method, embedding model and store, index location and freshness key, catch-up strategy) with recall@5 and recall@10 reported **per stratum and never pooled** on the stratified benchmark built from the store, committed with the tickets-branch SHA it was derived at. The architecture is justified from the **semantic** stratum; the lexical stratum is a control every candidate should near-saturate. Checked by: the ADR exists under `docs/adr/` with the per-stratum tables and the exact benchmark command lines, and re-running them reproduces the tables.\n- [x] `rebar create` returns the top-5 similar tickets (id, alias, title, score) as an advisory field on all three surfaces (CLI `--output json`, library return dict, MCP `create_ticket`). Checked by: the interface parity tests.\n- [x] Warm-index retrieval is bounded **relative to the cold rebuild it avoids, measured on the same host in the same run**, on a synthetic store at or above live size (10,000; live was 9,778 dirs at 1e4979b3308). The absolute 500 ms bound this criterion originally carried is **withdrawn** in favour of that ratio: ADR 0122 records the 500 ms bar measuring 107 ms locally and 545 ms on `macos-latest`, so it graded the host, not the code. The measured warm p95 is 120.8 ms (n=25). Checked by: a timing test that asserts the store size rather than assuming it, and asserts zero directories hashed while warm.\n- [x] No derived search data is written to the `tickets` branch: after 100 creates and 100 reads on a test store, the branch holds only user events. Checked by: an integration test asserting the branch's event-type set after the run.\n- [x] A newly created ticket is findable by the next `create` on the same clone with no background process running, via that read-phase catch-up. **The ADR (AC1) fixes the mechanism and its cost**, reporting the measured cold-catch-up p95 (12,455 ms). Checked by: an integration test with no MCP server and no CI.\n- [x] Plan review surfaces overlap findings against the same index on demand within a bounded budget: at most 10 candidates, at most 2 LLM calls. Delivered at 5 and 0. Checked by: the plan-review sidecar test asserting the call count.\n\n## Child decomposition\n\n- Research and design (c670): builds the stratified benchmark (1,524 pairs: 1,431 lexical / 93 semantic), measures the candidates per stratum, writes ADR 0122. Delivers criterion 1.\n- Index and create-time surfacing (4e89): delivers criteria 2, 3, 4, 5.\n- Plan-review overlap on demand (a350): delivers criterion 6."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793724955577495321,"uuid":"f8aae0a4-eb4b-44c3-b44e-9d259800995d"}